for the right to use the computer facilities and when that right
is granted (account number issued) it is granted with the
understanding that the person's use of the computing system will
correspond to the purpose(s) stated by the user in making his
request.

     At GWU, academic users are not personally billed
for, but are held personally responsible for, their proportion of
the associated costs.  Therefore, certain practices are
considered a misuse of University property.  Examples of such
misuse are: the use of any part of the computing system for
personal profit or the provision of free resources to unauthorized
users both within and without the university community.
     Security is a much discussed and misunderstood topic.  It is
not our intent to discuss it fully here but rather to give some
comments regarding computer security at GWU.

     It is considered a generally acceptable purpose among users to
learn about the workings of the operating system.  The operating
system is that program which controls the tasks in the computer or
network, makes its services available to the many users, and
controls access to these services.  Since all users are in some way
dependent upon the operating system, it is an area where interest
must be accompanied by personal caution and integrity.  As a
person learns more about the capabilities of the operating
system, he/she discovers new ways to access data, and in some
cases, gains the knowledge to look at, modify, or destroy the work
of others.

     The basic point we are making is that there is no reasonable
way to prevent the misuse of the computer within our environment
except through the personal integrity of each individual user.
If a person is given the freedom to learn, he/she must understand
that freedom implies responsibility.  Only each individual user
can assure the proper use of the computer.

     There is no fool-proof security system.  The major security
and privacy mechanism that we have lies in the internals not of the
operating system but of people.  GW is dedicated to educating its
students in a manner which encourages them to make their own
responsible decisions while understanding that they must be held
accountable for their decisions.

     In keeping with the idea that academic interest ceases when it
invades the privacy of others or becomes an irresponsible use of
a scarce resource, the following, (but not limited to the
following) is considered improper on our systems:

     Frivolous use of computers.
     
     Inspection of data or functions which are neither allotted to
     the user's account, nor specified as public.

     Inspection of data which have to do with computer
     utilization, authorization for computer access, or security.

     Modification of data which are not specifically assigned to
     or created by the modifier.

     Use of another's account number, or logon ID.

     Interference with other users of the system.
     
     Destruction of data or property which is not owned by the
     destroyer.

     Use of another's programs without his/her permission.

     Sending obscene or vulgar messages.

     Abusive or improper use of hardware or public software.

     When a student or other user is learning new techniques
sometimes he/she may degrade the system performance or cause other
users hardships of which the learner is unaware.  If a user has any
doubt about using a programming technique, program, or command,
it is best to refer to the CIRC Technical Assistance office for
verification of the method.

     University computing centers routinely cancel accounts, bring
criminal charges, or take other serious action against users who
act in an irresponsible manner and infringe the right of others.
At GWU we have used these measures when necessary in order to
preserve the security and proper operation of the computer
center.

