> The definition of "government" in the US is accurate, but what
> does "government" mean in Canada?

Roughly, the currently governing party (which must, by custom which has the
force of law, command enough votes in the legislature to govern). The
government consists of the cabinet (part of both legislature AND executive),
the Privy Council (Executive), and the most senior ranks of the civil service
(the clerk of the privy council). It does NOT include the more junior ranks
of the civil service, particularly the law enforcement echelons, which our
law and tradition stricly excludes from the political side of government (for
obvious reasons).

None the less, politics has had an effect on the directions Canada customs
has taken, since the Progressive Conservative party (governed for the past
nine years) saw the issue of pornography as a nice "bipolar" issue (appealing
to the left through Feminism and Children's rights, and the right through
"conventional morality"). As a result, they pushed it rather hard, including
measures (taken in the last months of their term) aimed at cyberspace (the
law on posession of child pornography relates specifically to computer BBSes).

