I don't know if I would call what happened to Ezekiel censorship.  No one
has deprived him of the right to express his views, or incarcerated him
for them - he has merely been told that a medium for which someone else
foots the bill [this being the key] does not wish him to use it specifically
to harass other people.

I think that if he were sending hate mail to Hispanics, for example, on
the basis of their ethnicity he would be in a similar stew.

The case is complicated for him by the fact that he broke (apparently)
established rules of ethics and conduct that all GWU students agree to by
being students.  At Harvard (where I teach) and Berklee College of Music
(where I'm a student) we have similar rules - the differnce between freedom
of expression and harassment drawn between arguing ideological lines and
attacking particular people for their beliefs or ethnicity.  Since his
behavior clearly fell into the latter category (especially with the
name-calling, et. al.) I have no real problem with the fact that he lost
his computer accounts for failing to adhere to the standards of conduct
the university has set.

Now, Ezekiel can easily set up his own computer system, and pay for the 
phone and power and all that and say anything he wants, and no one should
stop him from doing so.  Then it is a private matter between Ezekiel,
his conscience, and the First Admendment.  The school would have no say
in the matter, because he would not be using school resources to promote
his views.

These rules are generally not drawn up by a secret society, either.  At
Berklee, Harvard, and MIT (where I went for a couple of years) they were
part of the Student Handbook - the policy was explicitly stated and
made abundantly clear in orientation meetings and in print.  Like any
other University policy, if one chooses to violate it, one must pay the
consequences.

Yes, you are right.  Ezekiel will probably learn nothing, and most likely
this experience will only embitter him further.  But frankly, everyone
tried to be reasonable with him, to persuade him to at least not
attack people.  People even asked him to leave, because he was offending
a great percentage of the readership, and singling them out one by one.
I don't know why he stayed around or what he hoped to accomplish, but 
I find it refreshing that while he may continue to have the same views
he has always had, at least I won't have to have Leviticus read to me
anymore.

					larry...


